-- `Shared` has two named-field constructors that AGREE on every offset and on every
-- declared field TYPE — a shape `main` currently has no declaration-level check against
-- at all (a proposed `T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT` guard on the still-parked PR #1455 would
-- deliberately leave it legal too, because the LLVM backend emits one constant slot
-- index and the choice of stamp is unobservable there).
--
-- On PR #1455's branch this file backed a two-fixture WITNESS pair proving the divergent
-- set is not statically characterizable at the typecheck seam: this same `zshared.mdk`
-- byte-for-byte, paired with an entry module that consumes `mkSB` via `match` instead of
-- projecting `.sx`, is WasmGC-clean. That witness fixture belongs to #1455's typecheck
-- work and is not ported here — only #1469's own pin (this directory) is.
--
-- On WasmGC the divergence IS observable: a record is a `struct` type PER CONSTRUCTOR and
-- `emitFieldAccessRef` (`compiler/backend/wasm_emit.mdk`) emits
-- `ref.cast (ref $C_<ctor>)` + `struct.get`, so casting an `SB` value to `SA`'s struct
-- type is illegal whatever the layouts are.  That is #1469, and it is a property of
-- the USE SITE, which is exactly what no declaration-level rule can see.
public export data Shared =
  | SA { sx : Int, sy : Int }
  | SB { sx : Int, sy : Int }

export mkSA : Shared
mkSA = SA { sx = 1, sy = 2 }

export mkSB : Shared
mkSB = SB { sx = 3, sy = 4 }
